{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat assignments/T-H-q8-activation-reuse.md'","aggregated_output":"# Assignment T-H (parent: campaign c1 / W3 engine candidate C1; orchestrator = Claude)\nHypothesis H-fuse-q8: in llama.cpp's CUDA backend, batch-1 decode with a Q8_0 model quantizes the SAME float activation to q8_1 several times per layer (observed kernel order, artifacts/profiles/q8_tg128_node.sqlite and campaign D008/D011): rms_norm -> quantize_q8_1 -> mul_mat_vec_q (Q) -> rope -> quantize_q8_1 -> mul_mat_vec_q (K) -> quantize_q8_1 -> mul_mat_vec_q (V); and in the MoE FFN one quantize feeds the gate/up expert matvecs. ~145 quantize_q8_1 launches/token take 14.4% of GPU kernel time (avg 1.6 us each, each on the critical path). If q/k/v (and any other consumer of the same src1 tensor) reuse ONE quantization, ~2 launches/layer disappear (expected ~3-4% decode gain, to be measured).\nInputs: llama.cpp source /home/muk/projects/siminfeng/campaign/ref/llama.cpp @31288c9c6 (READ ONLY; produce a patch only). Relevant: ggml/src/ggml-cuda/{ggml-cuda.cu,mmvq.cu,mmvq.cuh,quantize.cu,quantize.cuh,common.cuh}; the CUDA graph path (ggml_cuda_graph_*, ggml_backend_cuda_graph_compute); ggml_cuda_pool_alloc (RAII, LIFO-scoped pool).\nHard correctness requirements (this is the \"stale activation reuse\" hazard class):\n - Reuse ONLY when the consumer's src1 is the very same ggml_tensor* (and same data pointer, type, ne/nb, and same quantization params) as an earlier quantization in the SAME graph evaluation on the SAME stream/context, and no node between producer and consumer can have overwritten src1's buffer (ggml graph tensors are SSA, but the ggml allocator reuses buffers: you must make sure the cache is invalidated when a different node writes that data pointer, or restrict reuse to consumers that are adjacent/within a safe window — explain the rule you chose and why it is safe).\n - The cache must be reset at the start of every ggml_backend_cuda_graph_compute call (and be correct under CUDA graph capture/replay: topology is fixed per captured graph; explain how replay with updated kernel params stays correct).\n - The reused q8_1 buffer must outlive the first consumer: ggml_cuda_pool_alloc buffers are freed at scope exit, so the cache needs a context-owned (persistent) buffer or an extended lifetime; do NOT keep a dangling pool pointer.\n - Applies to batch size 1 (mul_mat_vec_q) path first; also cover the mul_mat_id (MoE) vec path if straightforward, otherwise state it is not covered. Must be a no-op (identical behavior) for all other types/paths. Add an env switch GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE (default ON in the patch, =0 disables) so A/B is possible from one binary.\n - Output numerics MUST be bit-identical to the unpatched backend (same quantization kernel, same data).\nWrite ONLY under: /home/muk/projects/siminfeng/campaign/cc/q8_reuse/ and artifacts/codexH/.\nDeliver: (1) cc/q8_reuse/q8-activation-reuse.patch (git unified diff, `git apply --check` clean against a COPY of the 31288c9c6 tree, e.g. `git archive` to /tmp); (2) cc/q8_reuse/DESIGN.md: rule for safe reuse, lifetime handling, graph-capture reasoning, what is NOT covered, risks; (3) cc/q8_reuse/README.md: how to apply via the Build CC (`--patch`), the A/B env switch, and the checks that must pass (bit-identical logits: llama-perplexity --kl-divergence-base on baseline vs patched Q8_0 must give mean KLD 0 and 100% same top-1).\nYou cannot run Docker or CUDA here. The orchestrator will run a nvcc compile check against your patch and send you any compile errors; so keep the change small, self-contained and conservative. Time limit 40 min. Final message: files written, the reuse rule, open risks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
